Z||000016||DJRice||04-10-2001||11:46 AM||||The Chinese are full of crap.  Period.  Everyone knows full well that the Chinese pilots were playing Top Gun and bumped that E-3.  No doubt the E-3 knew they were there, but was probably ignoring them.  I would bet that this type of showoff flyby stuff happens on a weekly if not daily basis, but there has never been a collision.<P>My take on it is screw pride and bring the soliders home.  The damage is done, if any of the equipment on that plane was left intact after they landed, the Chinese have had more than a week to play with it.  If they want an apology, give it to them.  The rest of the world knows who to blame when a Jet fighter hits a propellor driven modified cargo plane.  Once the crew of the plane is home, enact a trade embargo and tell China to forget about joining the World Trade Organizaion until they grow up.  After all, we can live without all the supermarket/convience store junk toys for a few months, right?  <P><P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<P>"Who wants somma Debo?"||||reg||1
Z||000017||Oreif||04-10-2001||11:47 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR><B>I heard that the Chinese pilot who did this was notorious for being a "hotdog" pilot and has pulled stunts like this before.  That was on CNN, not sure how accurate but I thought it was interesting...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I had heard the same thing. I also know for a fact that All fighter pilots, regardless of country, are hot dogs at heart. Think about it for a moment. Someone who is in their mid 20's (average) with a highly manuverable, mach + jet. It's like giving all the mid 20 year olds a Fiero GT with a built 350 V-8 in it. (hell I'm in my 30's and I'd still "show off"!!!)<P>||||reg||1
