Z||000032||Mach10||04-10-2001||11:51 PM||||First off: I agree with Mothergoose. Let's not be racist assholes here, huh? "Chink" is VERY offensive to me, as a white caucasian male. I hang my head in shame. If it was a Freudian Slip, Fine, whatever. Apologize and move on.<P>Now, Let's go over the whole incident:<P>1) International airspace or not, it was still a SPY PLANE. Tell me that you are not going to be offended if your neighbor is watching you shower with a telescope. Now tell me you aren't going to march over there and start screaming and flailing your arms<P>2) IT WAS AN ACCIDENT. I'm 99.99% sure that neither pilot wanted to hit each other. I don't know airspace regulations, but it would seem that the rule of Mass applies: Bigger, slower has right of way. However, the fact remains that YOU were spying on THEM. A person caught spying on the mainland would probably have been executed.<P>Having said that, I don't believe that there IS a right/wrong to this issue. The US was spying, China was Hot-dogging. An accident ensued. China currently has something of yours, and is holding it (and people) ransom. YES. It is a STUPID CHILDISH GAME, but while they hold the things that you want, play by their rules. No honor is lost to apologizing for a problem that is 50% your fault. Come ON Bush, be a man.<P>Please don't go say something like "Bomb the hell out of them." That would be a VERY BAD THING. Here's a short (  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">) essay why:<BR>First off, the US would LOSE a war with China. Outnumber us 4-1? Think bigger. A LOT bigger.  True, China could never occupy the US, but they could grind you into a thin, watery meat paste. Remember, there are several types of war. Resource-based (we want your oil), territory based (this was our land, we want it back), and culturally based (Your people must be exterminated). The differences between them are extreme. In both resource and territorial wars, care would be taken to preserve the infrastructure. In a cultural war, which is what this would be, emphasis is on mass destruction. Anything and everything is a target.<BR>a) China has almost unlimited natural resources, and a HUGE fully conscriptable population base. The US has an "Inpenetrable Air Sheild" Inpenetrable to many attacks? Yes. I'd guess that it'd take maybe 2, 3 years at the most for the chinese air force to TOTALLY wear out the defences. Then, they'd start shipping people, and machinery. The US would be completely overrun in 10 years or less. China's war-machine will keep running that long. Freedom of speech does not really exist, so the population would not be learning about the horrific losses that China WILL sustain (your guns ARE better afterall  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">). Instead, they would be informed that "we are making progress." and more people would be conscripted to swell the ranks.<BR>b) EVERYONE would take sides in a conflict like this, and sorry to say it, but not too many would side with the US. Take the former USSR. They have more in common with china than the US. (1700 Calories/day under Communism, 1100 Calories/day under Democracy) Most likely, they'd fence-sit for a while, but as things started to turn in China's favor, they'd team up with them. So now you have THE LARGEST LAND MASS gunning for your extinction. High-tech or not, you will NOT last against an unending onslaught. Yes, your guns are better, but given a choice of one machine gun versus 100 muskets, I'd take the muskets. Remember. You only have a SMALL portion of the population in the army. China would be able conscript ANYONE. Their system of government is MUCH better set up for full-scale conflict than yours. Yours is set up for profit. (OH, and BTW, cutting trade with china will not change things too much. they have all the resources they need right at home. The peasants might get a little hungrier, that's all)<BR>c) American mentality. Your population(and ours) is COMPLETELY psychologically unprepared for full-out conflict. As soon as China started attacking, most of your production would halt, the population would seize up, and the US would have to commit a good number of forces JUST TO STABILIZE THE POPULOUS. Not good when fighting for your lives. <BR>c)<BR>That's WITHOUT Nuclear intervention. With Nuclear intervention, as soon as the first Polaris (or whatever) took off, every nuclear power would open up too. There'd be very little military on ANY side after that.<P>So lets take stock:<BR>You will be fighting an army that a) is larger, b) better organized (more scared of their superiors than of you, and kept in the dark) c) better funded (china can make EVERYTHING they need at home, using cheap conscripted labor) and d) without morals or scruples (On the warpath. Nothing has to be left standing, so nothing likely will)<P>*WHEW*<BR>Did I make any errors here? <BR>I'm not Anti-US. I'm anti-war. A war with China would benefit no-one. As I said, china would CRUSH the US's army, but if they tried to occupy, the survivors, having taken to the hills, will be CONSTANTLY taking chunks out of the main force. It would end up being too troublesome to continue an occupation, and china would eventually pull out.<P>So, in conclusion, yeah, I'm kind of pissed off by the whole thing. I hope that Bush does the right thing. I think that they are on the right track, by keeping good communications open. Neither side is all that concerned about what's going on. It *IS* a game after all   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Oh, and *IF* China decided to invade, they'd come through Canada, so I'd be just as much up a **** -creek as you all.<P>Before I end, bear in mind that I THOUGHT about these responses, and tried to keep it reasonably clean. If someone wants to take me up on my facts, be my guest, and argue. I love's a good argument. Please don't resort to petty name-calling   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>If you question my lineage, at least have some DNA evidence   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Let the flaming begin!<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 04-10-2001).]||||reg||8
Z||000033||DRH||04-11-2001||12:20 AM||||Mach10,<P>This type of "spying" is standard procedure for a lot of countries. This was not an unusual mission, it happens on a regular basis. The Chinese government knows that... the E3 is hardly "stealth" by ANY stretch of the imagination. The fighters were sent out to harass the E3, not try to capture it or run it off.<P>The main reason the US is listening to conversations in that area is to try to make sure we are aware of any military build up because of Taiwan. If China would quit rattling their sabers about that issue, maybe we wouldn't be so interested in what they have to say amongst their military people.<P>I think the big problem here is that before this incident the Chinese PEOPLE didn't know this type of thing went on. THEY are pissed, and their government is forced to take a hard line. <P>It's mostly political manuevering... It'll calm down soon.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000034||RNGCRSH||04-11-2001||12:36 AM||||Hit them where it hurts, Give all 52,000 Chinese nationals in this country US citizen ship. this way the knowledge they have gained in our universities at tax payer exspence won't go back to China.<p>[This message has been edited by RNGCRSH (edited 04-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000035||Oreif||04-11-2001||12:42 AM||||To start with, China could never "invade" the U.S. At present China does not have the equipment nor resources to sustain an overseas war. In order for the size of their Army to make a difference, They would have to move the Army. Also the U.S. does not have the ability to invade China. Moving Armies to fight with each other (China/U.S.) can only be done by sea or air. Our carrier force is the largest around. They could not get their ships near the U.S. and Planes are limited by distance. China has very few of the large global transports planes to do the job. Remember most of China's equipment was purchased from Russia. Do you think Russia gave them top-of-the-line stuff? Most of China's military equipment is older Russian equipment. The T-34 tanks were used in WW II. The U.S. doesn't even sell equipment to it's allies that is as good as our own. Yes it may be the same plane but the electronics inside is very different. As for U.S., China has a very large submarine fleet. Although none are nuclear missle subs, They would wreck havoc on any fleet. China has a large Air Force of fighters, Trying to gain Air supremecy would also be difficult. China could not send their subs here due to lack of ability to replenish underway. (all of them are the smaller K-class, alpha, and kilo attack subs) Neither country could sustain any conventional war without the help of allies. In the present circumstances, no other country would join either side. There is no way either side could build-up forces close enough to the other without anyone knowing.<BR>Russia has there own problems to worry about. Also the South East Asia Treaty Organization (SEATO) would give each side enough allies to turn it into a global world war. It would never come to that over this latest incident.<BR> <BR>Next, your "american mentallity" won't cut it. The Japanese thought that would happen with Pearl Harbor, Hitler thought it would happen in Britain with the V-1 and V-2. Hitler even thought turning on Russia would cause enough fear to take Moscow. (didn't happen) Even the British thought it would happen with the american colonists. Attacking and expecting the countries populace to bow down is foolish, usually it incites them. Remember when Russia stormed into Afganastan? Same principle.<BR> <P>You are under-estimating the U.S.'s ability and over-estimating China's. Each country has it's strength and weaknesses. There is a huge difference between the size of an Army and what it is capable of doing. If any kind of "war" comes out of this it'll be a "cold war" just like we had with Russia, All a big political battle.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 04-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000036||Black88GT||04-11-2001||01:35 AM||||Very good reading Oreif.  <P>About "Mach10's" second point, yes it was a spy plane, however every country knows that spying is commonplace and nothing to attack over.  Remember Los Alamos recently?  Highly classified &lt;missing&gt; nuclear secrets ring a bell?<P>Also, about Chinese being able to produce everything they need at home, well what can we not produce at home?  Ok maybe Oil, but we have TONS of oil stashes placed all over the world so we could last a while on those oil reserves, atleast long enough to hold back an invasion.<P>That American Mentality theory is crap too.  Like Oreif pointed out, people have done that in history before and look where it got them.  Americans united over the Gulf war I am sure if someone invaded we would make our peace with differing orginizations quickly.<P>For the sake of argument, why would China come through Canada anyway even if they could move all of those people in a very short amount of time?  Mexico makes more sense.  But like Oreif said there is no way they would be able to move that many people considering we have the best Navy/Air Force.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Black88GT (edited 04-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000037||Kelvin Vivian||04-11-2001||03:39 AM||||Well here's my take, and chances are it's totally off base, but then again, nothing is impossible:<P>1) US bombed Iraq for its agressions.<BR>2) Russia and China exclaimed to the United Nations that the US acted out of line, the European Union did not condone the bombings.<BR>3) US then has a Russian spy scandal. Says to UN, look what the Russians are doing!<BR>4) China responds by intercepting one of our planes and now says, well look what the US is doing!<P>If the events above are inter-related, then I will guess that if China doesn't return the US citizens, and Bush doesn't apologize, then we will bomb Iraq again. Make sense? <P>I don't think our citizens will be mis-treated, it would defeat China's political interest/purpose; it is their purpose to have the US admit that it made a mistake, regardless if it was our fault or not.<P>I was only 6 in 1980... didn't we go through a similar circumstance with hostages in Iran around 1980. Instead of negotiating, President Carter decided to send in a special military unit to rescue our citizens, but the unit ran into severe weather and the mission failed. I think it ended with Reagan exchanging arms for the hostages...||||reg||3
Z||000038||Mach10||04-11-2001||04:22 AM||||Tears of pride roll down my cheeks! I had faith in you guys! I was not disapointed! Not a single "your mom" flame! Intelligent answers! Very well done!<BR> <BR>Before I continue, a few points:<BR> <BR>Yes, I agree. Any hostilities will result in a Cold War. Too expensive to wage war on another continent. Also, let's assume that nuclear/chemical weapons will be excluded. This argument was mostly sparked by the "Let's go bash some skulls" attitude that some people (not necesarily here) seem to be adopting. The arguments here are ASSUMING that for whatever reason, China wants the US wiped off the map, no intention of annexation or occupation.<BR> <BR>But I digress! On with the fray!<BR> <BR>DRH: Standard procedure, yes... No-one likes it when the spy is caught. I repeat, if they were land-based spies, they'd probably have been executed. I agree, it'll settle down. Thing you have to remember, though, is that the chinese people have no say in what goes on. Population of several BILLION, most of which are peasant-class workers. News doesn't get around much. I will not argue that the spying was legitimate.<BR> <BR>RNGCRSH: LOL!!!<BR> <BR>Oreif: I disagree. Your air/naval sheild is very strong (strongest in the world), but it will NOT last forever. China has almost unlimited resources, and the largest workforce available. They can and would produce the number of planes and amunition required. It would take a while, and horrific losses on the part of the chinese, but eventually, they'll punch through. The idea here, is that under communism, information would be withheld, much as it was in the USSR. The people would be told "We are making progress." and there would be no politcal backwash from the populace. When funds get low, they squeeze the peasants. Not nice.<BR>As for the American mentality, you overestimate Joe American's ability to cope. At first, after the Chinese airforce is busy smashing itself to pieces on your Airshield, things will be ok. However, when things start hitting home, society will start to break down. The capitalist society that makes up your political and social structure is not built to deal with heavy losses. Vietnam, for example. Political upheaval will start, small ripples at first, gradually growing bigger. Trade will halt, as all the investors duck and cover. Breakdown of basic services will ensue as the US starts fighting harder, and commiting more resources to keep china out. I still think that china has more than enough resources to cover an invasion. The US doesn't. Natural resources abound down there, but as war gets more involved, industry will start closing in on itself. To maintain a stream of supplies, the US government would be forced to institute martial law. For most, the loss of basic freedom would be too much. Look how riled up everyone down there gets on the gun-control issue. Martial law would cripple the country. Full scale conflict is VERY stressful. In WW-II, wars had been going on and off for the last 100 years. The society was emotionally equiped to deal with it. It's now 50 years later. <BR>Vietnam was a disaster for all involved, and the effects hit hard at home. Social unrest is bad for a country. The US took a beating, but media coverage softened the blow somewhat. Don't forget that the conflict was far from home, the distance cushioning the effect somewhat. This kind of attack would see the chinese moving in, close to home, and pushing the skirmish line closer in. That kind of strain WILL break the country. It'd happen up here too. All over North America. Our (north american/western civs) "free" society has no emotional provisions for life-death struggle. We have no way of dealing with it, most of us not having any experience with it. It's not the Army that would falter. Comparing a socialist society to a democratic society is not really a good idea. Consider that in a communist society (the real one, not Marxist  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">) you have no rights, freedom of speech, freedom of information. The military holds power, and you have lived all your life like this. You probably have already been conscripted for basic training, so are a soldier too. This kind of person will do as he/she is told, and will believe his/her government's word absolutely. In our society, we can and do question our government's word. "Don't Panic" coming from the whitehouse sends waves of anxiety throughout the populace. Case and point: Meningococcus(sp?) outbreak in Winnipeg. Doctors quote that there is a small chance of contracting the virus. result? Mass immunization of the entire student youth... Parents not letting children go to school etc. Out of a city of 600,000, we have had 6 cases, most are isolated. Yet it caused mass panic. Now picture the threat of 3 billion irate chinese soldiers marching in. The entire society would be quaking in it's boots. Our freedom does have it's downside. The examples you quoted are good, but have one small flaw: Every one of those examples come from an era which was full of mass conflict. WW-II came just after WW-I. Still more or less in the same generation. "We did it before, and we can do it again!" was the general attitude. It's been 40 years since the last major conflict. Gulf War was not much of an issue. The US was on the offensive and had all those cool LGB20 videos showing microscopic precision and reporting no losses to reassure the population that all was well. How are you going to comfort the masses when you are busy shooting down a sky full of Chinese fighters? We live in a TOTALLY different society now from back then. It's hard to scare someone with the prospect of war if they've already been through it. How many reading this who are not in the military have first-hand experience with warfare (besides a parade  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)? I thought not. Again, the military will be doing it's job.<BR>Size of army? Point taken. Very hard to command 10 million troops at once. So lets say that you send them over on 10k chunks? They die. But they make a small impression. <BR>Next set. and the next, and the next and so on. Anyone ever play StarCraft? Think Zergs. Having a 3:1 Kill ratio (3 of yours to 1 of them) is considered playing conservatively. Same thing here. The idea is that you don't wear down an army in one go, you whittle away at it. If it came to the US and China butting heads, who do you think would run out of money and people first?<BR>*whew*<BR>Hats off to Orief. Good argument....<P>HAVE TO STOP FOR A MOMENT. WOULD NOT LET ME POST THIS LARGE! MORE COMING!<BR>---To be Continued---||||reg||10
Z||000039||Mach10||04-11-2001||04:25 AM||||---Continued---<BR> <BR>Black88GT: See above: Spying is tolerated, as long as you aren't caught with your pants down. As for Chinese/America production? YES! Good point! You can produce EVERYTHING you need at home. My point is that they can produce much, MUCH more, and faster. And have people left over to drive whatever they built! <BR>Yes, the US united over the GW. However, that confrontation lasted 6 weeks. Not to mention that the US was just PUMMELLING Iraq, LGB mishaps or not. The US had a clear target: A Sadistic Megalomaniacal dictator, not to mention "coming to the aid of a weaker nation"(with it's own megalomaniacal dictator, but that's another story). Combine the two with the short lived combat, and it's fairly easy to deal with. How many people were scared of the Iraqis coming over the hills with machine guns? Thought not. What I am talking about is a full-scale conflict ON OR NEAR american soil, and moving INWARD. Having the best navy/airforce will only stall them. My point is that if they wanted to come in, they will. Slowly, and with much cost to human life, they will. Try stopping a bulldozer with  pickup truck. Yeah, the Pickup is faster, and has air-conditioning, and TONS of power, but when something slow, heavy, and very powerful starts pushing, the only direction is backwards.<BR>Canada is the logical entry point. We have TONS of uninhabited space to put an army, not to mention the fact that we are the weak link. We have almost NO army to speak of. Not being able to defend ourselves is actually a security issue for the US  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Mexico does have a stronger presence... (there is ONE problem to coming through us... Anyone figure it out?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)<P>--To Be Continued-- ||||reg||1
Z||000040||Mach10||04-11-2001||04:26 AM||||--Continued--<BR>I think I should pause here and make it clear that I am not calling Americans cowards. My wording was a little wrong. It's actually all of western civilization, and the way our society is structured. Humans base their entire existance upon experience, and VERY few of us know about live combat. The sudden change, and ensuing pressure will eat away at us. I know *I* would not be able to function properly knowing that there's a possibility that we may be invaded. Can you say "Panic Attack"? Think about the effect that extended stress has on people. Especially violent stress. I could go on and on about "Seyle's Adaptation Syndrome" but as I just wrote a psych exam (shudder) I won't unless provoked. Instead, consider Post Traumatic Stress Syndrome. Now imagine a whole country full of sufferers. Not good. That does remind me of a good point, which I will get to later  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR> <BR>About the subject of alliances: Human nature will always endure. Alliances will be created/dropped according to the best interests (or percieved interests) of those involved. I'm willing to bet that Russia would side with China. China and Russia have more in common with each other than with the US. At the least, I believe that Russia would fence sit, and remain neutral. Yes, they have their own problems. Army is almost defunct (except in quelling internal struggle. &gt;:|). However, even if they side with China, they don't have to take action. Simply allowing China to pass through will create a HUGE advantage for the Chinese, as then they only have to swim 34kms across the Bering sea. Now, lets say that China concentrates it's air-sheild puncturing here, with diversionary (very large) strikes further south. The US would be forced to stretch it's forces, and China would have a sizeable army on Canadian soil in a few weeks. This is purely hypothetical, but it's what I'd do  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Now! Here's the problem. Canadian terrain is not particularly forgiving. Especially to a people that live in a temparate climate. I wouldn't expect a good survival rate. Unfortunately, 10% of 10 million (assuming 90% mortality) is still 1 million. That's a hell of an army to have on your own turf. So now it's a Ground war. Well, the Chinese are hopelessly out-gunned (Ever see the Mask? Last scene, with the rocket launchers  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">). But again, here's a problem. The US CAN NOT start peppering the chinese army. This would be their own land. You can't go shooting up your own cities. The population would not like this. And so, with a steady stream of chinese coming across, the US would eventually be overrun.<BR> <BR>Now for the good news. The best that China could ever hope to do would be to destroy your military, and your central government. After that, the population would take to the hills. At this point. the shock of being at war would be over. Now, people will start to regroup, and "come together" as so many have stated. Most of the US is armed, or knows where to get arms. Guerilla warfare will ensue. China would not be able to hold out against this. Too expensive. The Chinese army would know that "they won" and would no longer have that trusting bravery stemming from government propaganda. It's easier to kill an army than hold out against a population that is TOTALLY at home in the environment. That's what happened in all the colonial wars. England won the major battles, but the armies were systematically dismantled by raids and attacks. <BR> <BR>*WHEW!*<BR>||216.81.4.207||reg||1
Z||000041||87ECO5||04-11-2001||09:15 AM||||To be quite honest, I'm extremely concerned by this whole thing.  I like to use humor to deal with fear:<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/USApology.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000042||JSocha||04-11-2001||09:35 AM||||Hey Mach!<P>The Americans I think will now give you the rest of the day off to get some sleep!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>And thank goodness for everyone (Americans/Canadians and of course Chineses alike)its "now" over;  They all B comin on home!<P>Who's going to officially close this thread?||||reg||1
Z||000043||firstfiero||04-11-2001||10:45 AM||||    You can't compare how the public reacted to vietnam to a conflict with china. The problem with vietnam was no one thought we should be their and that it wasn't any of our busness. Americans have no problem backing a war when we are directly attacked. Look at history...They knew that the jap. were going to attack pearl harbor. They allowed it to happen because they knew the only way america would back going to war was if they attacked our boys. These are two completly different situations that can't be compared.   <P>------------------<BR>Hey Mr. Ricer come over here and tell me how your stickers make you faster.....<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/headshot.gif"> <BR>Todd <BR>  86se||||reg||4
Z||000044||Oreif||04-11-2001||11:16 AM||||Mach10, You have some interesting points. But there are many factors you also need to take into account. First is experience. China has never been in a war where they had to use strategic air power or any naval battles. This is a handicap for China. Although they have the numbers they could never properly use them in an aggressive attack. As from the naval side, They may know how to operate a russian K-series sub, but they have no experience at sub warfare. They also have nothing larger than an cruiser for a surface ship. These smaller class ship's are not very effective as an attack force. All of the surface ships are regular fuel. So in order for them to get anywhere near U.S. waters they need to refuel. They cannot do underway replenishment. Which means the cannot do it while the ship is moving. They need to stop the ships. With the amount of support ships that China has, they could not send enough ships at one time to make a viable threat.  So if I was across an ocean and knew the above I'd wait until they were refueling and then attack the fleet with aircraft carriers. (China has no carriers) My destroyer screen and attack subs would keep their subs away. <BR>As for their Air Force, They have nothing long range and no aircraft carriers. So where are they going to land and refuel? China also has a very limited amount of strategic aircraft. So the only way they could effectively get here from there would be to "Island hop" across the Pacific. Which would require them to take-over the islands since we have more island alliesthan they do. This would cause other allies of the island countries to help defend them. (Taiwan is allied with us and Britain, The first island China would take-over would be them. This would bring Britain into the battle) Russia did not sell many types of planes and ships to China that could pose a threat to themselves. They learned that in World War II with Germany. China has a huge force but also they have never had a war where supply lines were needed. As an example, Look at Rommel in north africa in WW II. He had a large force with experienced troops and some of the best designed equipment back then. He drove the allies across the entire continent.<BR>But once his supply lines failed, he lost equipment and troops quickly. That was only 3500 miles. Do you really think China could supply their troops over 10,000 miles of ocean? They may have a large work force but no experience at mass producing weapons. They bought all of their equipment. Since they did not build them, reliability also becomes a large factor. Repairs take longer, some parts may be hard to find since Russia doesn't make the parts anymore. Now they need to make the parts themselves. So first they would reverse design the part, (to see how it works) then build their own.<BR>Or they would have to take the broken ones and use them to fix the operational ones. (HMM sounds like Fiero's have the same problem.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  ) <BR>So it all comes down to this, China has no experience at long range warfare. They do not have the proper equipment to do it nor the abilities required to maintain them over distance. Most of their equipment was bought for defense and is out-dated in terms of technical abilities. All of their experience/training is based on defending their own country. Waging a war across an ocean is very difficult and requires more than equipment and number of troops. If it was easy just to send fleets across, don't you think the U.S. and Russia would have done it before all this? The scuffles the U.S. had with Russia over the years were more intense. Both side knew that there was no way to effectively attack the other in a convetional war without a great sacrafice to thenselves. <BR>I am not saying that we would do any better as an aggressor. Many countries (including the U.S.) cannot afford to build "attacking" military. Everyone has built up the military for defense. That is one of the reasons we had to get many nations together to aid Kuwait. Notice it also took the better part of a year to get enough troops over there.<BR>Iraq had the equipment but not the experience to use it effectively. Do you really think the equipment used by China is different than the equipment used by Iraq? <P>As for Russia, They won't side with China. It is not in their best intrest. Once they come across the bearing sea towards Canada, they would now bring in more U.S. allies. Russia at present cannot afford a multi-front battle. Once Russia jumped in and Canada was threatened, NATO would be involved, Now Europe is involved. As for Taiwan, Just plain greed would cause them to take them over. Now that is in violation of SEATO. Hello Britain, Austraila, Korea, Japan, and New Zealand. Just because they (China and Russia) share the same politics does not mean they will join together. Even if we reverse the situation, The U.S. could not get there from here without stepping on someones toes and bring in other nations. It not as simple as it looks. <P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 04-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000045||Mach10||04-11-2001||11:40 AM||||firstfiero: Yes. No-one wanted to be there. Faced with 2 Billion (current population?) irate chinese, I can guarantee you that no-one will want to be there either  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I'm not comparing the conflict, just the socio-economic and pychological impacts.<P>Orief: Touche!<BR>Yes, I don't really have an answer for the long-range war. The only thing I can suggest is that if the Chinese government says "jump" the people jump, no questions asked. As for the logistics, a good friend of mine, heavy into this sort of thing (a fellow anthropologist  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)explained it as such:<BR>"Once china had theoretically decided that the world would be better off withouto us, they would sit down a say: 'What is the best way over' then build it"<BR>In reality, the ONLY advantages china has is an unlimited (well, close  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)resources and workforce. Their armies would be quite ineffective, most of the soldiers being farmers or workers.<BR>China would probably build a way over. Realistically, they COULD make a bridge made of human bodies from there to here  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>I stand by Russia teaming up with China. <BR>Russia still harbors animosity towards the US (remember the cold war?). US and china have been on good terms for centuries.<BR>Using Russia as a stepping stone would mean the end of the US in short order (along with CA with it) but yes: If nothing else, THIS action would drag EVERY other nation in with it. At this point, the outcome would be up in the air. In full-scale global(non-nuclear) warfare, the US would probably gain superiority fairly quick  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>It's been a blast!<BR>Mach10||||reg||6
